BioKids source package
======================

One source package, nine binary packages, one wheel. This file explains the
shape of the packaging, the decisions inside it that are not obvious from the
files, and - section 7 - exactly what could not be verified, because this
packaging was written on a Windows development host where dpkg-buildpackage
does not exist. Read section 7 before the first real build; it is a to-do list,
not a disclaimer.


1. Building
-----------

On a Debian 13 (trixie) machine or container, from the top of the source tree:

    sudo apt install build-essential devscripts debhelper dh-python \
        python3-all python3-setuptools pybuild-plugin-pyproject \
        po-debconf gnupg lintian
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
    lintian --fail-on error \
        --suppress-tags bad-distribution-in-changes-file \
        ../biokids_0.1.0-1_*.changes

`-b` (binary only) is deliberate, and it is what .github/workflows/ci.yml and
release.yml both do. The format is 3.0 (quilt), so building the *source*
package needs an orig tarball, which for this project is generated from the tag
rather than downloaded:

    git archive --format=tar.gz --prefix=biokids-0.1.0/ v0.1.0 \
        > ../biokids_0.1.0.orig.tar.gz
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -S

The suppressed lintian tag is explained in section 6.

If the tree was checked out on Windows, make sure debian/rules is executable
before building; .gitattributes keeps line endings LF, but the mode bit is a
separate thing:

    chmod +x debian/rules            # on the build host, or
    git update-index --chmod=+x debian/rules


2. How one wheel becomes nine packages
--------------------------------------

pyproject.toml describes a single project whose dependency list is the *union*
of every component's requirements: SQLAlchemy for the core, FastAPI for the
panel, dbus-next for the daemon. That is right for development and wrong for
installation - a machine that installs biokids-daemon should not acquire
FastAPI.

So:

  * debian/rules installs the whole wheel once, into debian/tmp
    (`dh_auto_install --destdir=debian/tmp`);
  * the .install files divide that tree between the packages;
  * `dh_python3 --no-guessing-deps` stops dh_python3 turning the wheel's
    metadata into ${python3:Depends}, and each binary package declares by hand,
    in debian/control, what it actually imports;
  * `dh_missing --fail-missing` makes sure nothing falls out of debian/tmp
    unclaimed. A module that silently stops being shipped is a package that
    silently stops working.

Three non-Python artefacts are produced in override_dh_auto_install rather than
being kept as extra files in debian/:

  * /usr/share/biokids/catalog/catalog.toml, copied from the fallback copy that
    lives inside biokids_core (D-20);
  * /usr/share/biokids/voice/<locale>/<event>.txt, copied the same way from
    biokids_core/data/voice. VOICE.md section 5 makes the lines data rather
    than code and names that path; biokids_voice.lines reads it before falling
    back to the copy inside the wheel, and its docstring says "which is what the
    .deb installs". They go into biokids-voice, not biokids-core: nothing else
    reads them;
  * /usr/share/biokids/alembic.ini, generated from the source tree's alembic.ini
    with script_location pointed at the installed package. Generated, not
    duplicated, so the two cannot drift. Neither copy contains a database URL:
    migrations/env.py asks biokids_core.config, which is what makes
    `alembic upgrade head` do the right thing in the postinst, in CI and under
    BIOKIDS_PREFIX without anyone editing an ini.

The gettext catalogues are compiled at build time by
`python3 dev/compile_i18n.py` in override_dh_auto_build and removed again in
override_dh_auto_clean. They are never committed: biokids_core.i18n falls back
to the .po when there is no .mo, which is what makes an edited string visible
to a developer immediately, and an installed system should not pay for that.


3. Units
--------

    debian/biokids-daemon.service                   -> biokids-daemon.service
    debian/biokids-daemon.biokids-lockdown.service  -> biokids-lockdown.service
    debian/biokids-panel.service                    -> biokids-panel.service
    debian/biokids-shell.user.service    -> /usr/lib/systemd/user/biokids-shell.service
    debian/biokids-voice.user.service    -> /usr/lib/systemd/user/biokids-voice.service

The lockdown unit's filename looks redundant and is not: `dh_installsystemd
--name=NAME` reads `debian/<binary package>.NAME.service` and has no fallback to
`debian/<binary package>.service`, so a file called plainly
`debian/biokids-lockdown.service` would name a binary package that does not
exist and would be ignored in silence, producing a daemon whose
OnFailure= points at nothing. The double prefix is what puts
biokids-lockdown.service inside biokids-daemon.

The same rule is why the daemon's own unit is NOT installed with
`--name=biokids-daemon`: that would look for
`debian/biokids-daemon.biokids-daemon.service`, find nothing, and produce a
daemon package containing no unit at all. It is installed by a second call that
names `biokids-daemon.service` as a positional argument instead. The argument is
not decoration either - dh_installsystemd otherwise acts on *every* unit already
in the package build directory, which by that point includes
biokids-lockdown.service, and the generated snippet would then start it on every
install. Passing the unit name keeps the enable/start code scoped to the daemon.

Three things in debian/rules exist to keep systemd from doing the obvious wrong
thing:

  * biokids-lockdown.service is installed with --no-enable --no-start, and the
    daemon's own call is scoped to one unit so that nothing re-enables it.
    Starting it locks every child session; it is triggered by OnFailure=, never
    by an apt run. It also has no [Install] section, on purpose.
  * The user units are installed with --no-enable. Enabling them globally would
    start a kiosk launcher inside the parent's own desktop. The child's session
    configuration enables them (T5.7).
  * biokids-daemon.service carries both `Restart=always` and
    `OnFailure=biokids-lockdown.service`, as D-106 requires, plus an
    ExecStopPost= that trips the lockdown directly. That third line is there
    because the first two do not compose the way they read: Restart=always
    revives the unit instead of letting it reach the failed state, so
    OnFailure= would not fire until StartLimitBurst is exhausted - five crashes
    later. T3.7 expects a single `systemctl kill -s KILL` to lock the session
    within five seconds, and the ExecStopPost is what delivers that. It is
    prefixed with `-` so that a lockdown which itself fails cannot turn a clean
    restart into a failed unit; OnFailure= remains as the second net.

The hardening block from SECURITY.md section 2 is reproduced in full in all
three system units. Two deliberate differences, both noted in the units
themselves:

  * ReadWritePaths entries for the three generated-config directories carry a
    `-` prefix in the daemon unit. systemd refuses to start a unit whose
    ReadWritePaths names a path that does not exist, and
    /etc/chromium/policies/managed only exists once Chromium is installed,
    which biokids-daemon does not require. Without the prefix, a daemon-only
    machine has a daemon that will not start.

    The prefix has a tail, and whoever builds the filter in Phase 1 needs to
    know it: a path skipped because it was absent at start does not become
    writable when it appears. Install Chromium on a running machine and the
    daemon still cannot write the managed policy until it is restarted. Either
    the filter code restarts the unit when it first renders a Chromium policy,
    or biokids-daemon co-owns that directory in a .dirs file. Do not let it
    become a filter that silently fails to apply.
  * biokids-panel gets /etc/biokids (it writes config.toml, which is the reason
    it runs as root) and does not get the three filter directories (the daemon
    is the single writer of a live filter, ARCHITECTURE.md section 2).

biokids-panel also declares RuntimeDirectory=biokids itself, with
RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes. /run is a tmpfs and ProtectSystem=strict makes it
read-only, so /run/biokids has to be in ReadWritePaths= - and a unit whose
ReadWritePaths= names a missing path refuses to start. On a machine where the
daemon has not started since boot, which is precisely when a parent needs the
panel to tell them why, that path does not exist. Declaring the runtime
directory creates it; Preserve=yes keeps stopping the panel from deleting the
directory the daemon's socket lives in.

The user units do not copy the hardening block. Most of it needs privileges a
user manager does not have, and the two directives that would apply are
harmful in a session: PrivateTmp= hides /tmp/.X11-unix, so the kiosk browser
would have no display, and ProtectHome= hides the home directory the child's
own files are in.


4. Maintainer scripts
---------------------

Only three packages have hand-written ones.

biokids-core.postinst creates the `biokids` group and the `biokids-ai` system
user, creates /var/lib/biokids (0700 root), /var/lib/biokids/evidence (0700
root), /var/log/biokids (0750 root:adm) and /run/biokids (0755), generates
/etc/biokids/ext.token (0640 root:biokids) and /etc/biokids/secret.key (0600)
if they are absent, runs `alembic upgrade head` against the shipped ini, and
seeds the application catalogue into the database with `biokids-admin
sync-catalog` - the same command a parent can repeat by hand, rather than a
second copy of the same logic embedded in a shell heredoc. It is idempotent
throughout: it runs again on every upgrade and on every `dpkg --configure`.

The three directories are created here rather than shipped in a .dirs file on
purpose. dpkg re-applies the recorded mode of a directory it owns on every
unpack, and 0700 on a family's history should not depend on a recorded mode
nobody looks at. /etc/biokids *is* shipped, so that dpkg owns it and takes it
away on purge once the generated secrets are gone.

Secrets are generated only when missing. Rotating secret.key would make every
stored SMTP password undecryptable; rotating ext.token would silently
disconnect the browser extension. secret.key is made by
biokids_core.security.load_or_create_key rather than by the shell, because a
Fernet key has a shape and because that helper opens the file 0600 from its
first byte.

biokids-daemon.prerm and biokids-panel.prerm stop their units, explicitly and
before the generated #DEBHELPER# block, so that the order is guaranteed rather
than inherited from whichever snippets debhelper happened to generate. A clean
stop is not a failure, so no session is locked because somebody ran apt.

biokids-daemon.postrm removes the generated dnsmasq, Chromium and nftables
files on purge. Those are not conffiles - they are rendered from the database
and carry a do-not-hand-edit header, and dpkg prompting an administrator about
a file no human wrote would be worse than useless - so dpkg does not know about
them and cannot clean them up itself. /etc/biokids/config.toml, by contrast, IS
a conffile: it is shipped under /etc from debian/etc/biokids/config.toml, dpkg
registers it automatically, and an edited copy is never overwritten. No
maintainer script in this package modifies a configuration file, so ucf is not
needed anywhere.

biokids-core.postrm is the purge question. A `remove` never touches
/var/lib/biokids. A `purge` asks, through debconf, and deletes only on a clear
yes; the logs follow the same answer rather than getting a second question. If
debconf cannot ask - an unattended purge, a frontend below the question's
priority, a system where debconf has already gone - the answer is no and the
data stays. That asymmetry is deliberate: a directory that could have been
deleted later is a nuisance, one that should have been kept is not
recoverable.

The question lives in debian/biokids-core.templates with its Turkish in
debian/po/tr.po, through po-debconf. debian/biokids-core.config asks nothing;
it registers the template at unpack time so an automated install can answer in
advance with debconf-set-selections, and clears the `seen` flag so a second
purge asks afresh. The extended description is deliberately one long physical
line: po-debconf reflows extended descriptions when it extracts them, and a
single line makes the msgid in the .po files unambiguous no matter how it
chooses to wrap.

The `biokids` group and the `biokids-ai` user are left behind on purge, per
Debian policy: when the data directory is kept something still owns it, and a
reissued uid inherits whatever files carried the old one.


5. Deliberate deviations from PACKAGING.md section 1
----------------------------------------------------

The dependency table in PACKAGING.md was written before some of this code
existed. Where this packaging differs, it is on purpose:

  * biokids-core also depends on python3-argon2 and python3-cryptography.
    biokids_core.security ships in core and imports both at module import time,
    so the table's `python3, python3-sqlalchemy, alembic` would produce a core
    package that cannot be imported. It also depends on adduser, which the
    postinst calls.
  * biokids-voice *recommends* piper-tts rather than depending on it. Piper may
    not exist under that name in trixie (section 7), and a hard dependency on a
    package apt cannot resolve would break `apt install biokids` entirely -
    which is the product's headline feature. It degrades correctly: the
    fallback chain in VOICE.md ends at an on-screen card, so a machine without
    Piper shows warnings instead of speaking them. Revisit this when T6.1
    settles how Piper is shipped.
  * biokids-desktop *recommends* the catalogue applications rather than
    depending on them. A parent who wants a smaller set should be able to
    remove GCompris without fighting apt, and nothing a parent has not enabled
    in the panel is shown to the child whether it is installed or not.
  * biokids-ai declares `Enhances: biokids-daemon` in addition to being only
    ever Suggested. It is never a Depends of anything, in either direction.
  * The table lists three dependencies per package and the code has more. The
    additions, all of them things the package imports or calls:
    biokids-daemon also gets python3-dbus-next (SessionWatcher, T3.1);
    biokids-panel also gets python3-jinja2, python3-multipart (every form in
    the panel is a FastAPI Form(), which needs it), python3-qrcode (the
    Tailscale step, T7.2), python3-itsdangerous, and biokids-daemon itself -
    the panel's three action buttons are the reason it exists and they are all
    calls into the daemon socket. python3-itsdangerous is the one with no
    importer today: nothing in biokids_panel imports it, because the session
    cookie is a server-side random token compared with hmac.compare_digest
    rather than a signed blob. It is here for Starlette's SessionMiddleware,
    which the panel does not currently use. Drop it if T4.2 settles without
    one.
  * Two Recommends the table does not mention: biokids-core recommends
    biokids-daemon and biokids-daemon recommends biokids-panel, so that
    installing any single piece by name gets a machine that actually does
    something. Neither is a Depends, so a deliberate partial install still
    works.


6. Before the first release
---------------------------

**The archive key.** debian/rules refuses to build if
`debian/biokids-archive-keyring.asc` is missing, and that is the intended
behaviour: a keyring package that ships no key is worse than one that will not
build.

To build the other eight packages before the key exists, use the build profile:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b -Ppkg.biokids.nokeyring

which drops `biokids-archive-keyring` from the build and skips the key entirely.
CI uses exactly that, so the packaging is verified on every push; the release
workflow does not, so a release that somehow lost the key fails loudly instead
of shipping eight packages and no way to trust them. Before the first release, generate a dedicated signing key (not a
personal one), commit its **public** half, ASCII-armoured, as
debian/biokids-archive-keyring.asc, and publish its fingerprint in README.md so
people can verify what they are trusting. The private half goes into GitHub
Secrets as GPG_PRIVATE_KEY and never touches this repository or a developer's
machine - see .github/workflows/release.yml, which is the only thing that ever
holds it, in a throwaway GNUPGHOME it destroys on the way out.

release.yml compares the fingerprint of the committed .asc against the key it is
about to sign with, and refuses to publish if they differ. The failure it exists
to prevent is quiet and late: publishing an archive signed with one key while
biokids-archive-keyring ships another breaks `apt update` on every machine that
trusted the package, weeks after the release looked fine, with an error that
reads like the archive has been tampered with.

**The lintian suppression.** debian/changelog says `trixie`, because that is
the suite name of this project's own archive, which is where these packages go.
lintian validates the Distribution field of a .changes file against Debian's
own list of suites and may call an unknown one an error, and a tag on a
.changes file cannot be overridden from inside the package. Hence
`--suppress-tags bad-distribution-in-changes-file` on the lintian invocations.
If it turns out lintian is happy with `trixie`, drop the flag - it is there to
keep one known-cosmetic tag from failing the build, and nothing else.
.github/workflows/ci.yml does not yet pass it.

There is no debian/watch. Nobody repackages BioKids from a tarball; the only
builds are from this git tree, and a watch file would be a maintenance
obligation with no reader.


7. What could not be verified on the development host
-----------------------------------------------------

This packaging was written on Windows 11. `dpkg-buildpackage`, `lintian`,
`piuparts` and `systemd-analyze verify` have never been run against it. Two
things *were* verified mechanically, against the actual source: that
debian/etc/biokids/config.toml matches every default in
src/biokids_core/config.py exactly, key for key and value for value; and that
the msgids in debian/po reassemble to exactly the text in
debian/biokids-core.templates.

Everything below is unverified, in roughly the order it will bite:

 1. **Provisioning a child now needs systemd, and that path has never run.**
    The panel cannot create a Unix account itself: ProtectSystem=strict makes
    /etc read-only and ProtectHome=yes mounts an empty tmpfs over /home, so
    `adduser` fails with EROFS and the child's dotfiles cannot be written. The
    privileged half therefore lives in biokids-provision@.service, and the panel
    reaches it with `systemctl start --wait biokids-provision@<child>.service`.

    Three things about that are unverified on this host, in order of risk:

      a. **Can the sandboxed panel talk to systemd at all?** Connecting to
         /run/systemd/private or the system bus from a unit with
         ProtectSystem=strict and SystemCallFilter=@system-service is expected to
         work - a read-only mount does not stop connect(2) on a socket - but it
         has not been tried. If it does not, the symptom is a child added in the
         panel with no Unix account and a problem row in the audit log saying so,
         which is at least honest. Check it first on the VM.

      b. **`systemctl start --wait` on a template.** The panel blocks on this for
         up to 150 s while a parent watches a spinner; the unit's own
         TimeoutStartSec is 120.

      c. **The instance name.** `provision_via_systemd` checks the name against
         UNIX_USER_RE and escapes `-` as `\x2d` before interpolating. Confirm
         with a hyphenated child that `systemd-escape` agrees.

 2. **The build has not been run, but the two things that used to block it are
    gone.** src/biokids_ai/__init__.py now exists, so the dh_install glob for
    biokids-ai matches, and src/biokids_shell/__main__.py exists, so the
    /usr/bin/biokids-shell console script points at a real module.
    `dev/check_packaging.py` now runs in CI and re-checks that class of mistake -
    every path a .install file names must exist or be produced by debian/rules -
    but it is a source-tree check, not a build.

 3. **Debian binary package names.** Every one of these was written from
    memory and must be checked with `apt-cache policy` on trixie before the
    first upload: python3-argon2, python3-cryptography, python3-dbus-next,
    python3-fastapi, python3-uvicorn, python3-jinja2, python3-multipart,
    python3-itsdangerous, python3-qrcode, piper-tts, gcompris-qt, tuxpaint,
    tuxmath, klavaro, stellarium, kolourpaint, supertux, frozen-bubble,
    gnome-mahjongg. python3-dbus-next and piper-tts are the two most likely to
    be wrong; if dbus-next is not packaged, that is a decision for T3.1 and not
    something to paper over here.

 4. **The dist-info glob.** biokids-core.install claims
    `usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/biokids-*`, which is the wheel metadata
    directory. With pybuild-plugin-pyproject that is `biokids-0.1.0.dist-info`.
    If the build falls back to the legacy setuptools path it will be an
    `.egg-info` instead - still matched by the glob, but check what actually
    lands there, because dh_install fails on a glob that matches nothing.

 5. **The systemd sandbox has never been started.** In particular:
    MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes against argon2-cffi and cryptography (cffi only
    needs W|X memory for callbacks, which neither creates, but this is worth
    five minutes of `systemctl start` on the VM);
    SystemCallFilter=@system-service against dbus-next's epoll and socket use;
    ProtectSystem=strict together with the ReadWritePaths lists;
    and the `$$SERVICE_RESULT` escaping in the daemon's ExecStopPost, which
    relies on systemd turning `$$` into a literal dollar before /bin/sh sees
    it. `systemd-analyze verify` on all five units is the cheapest first check.

 6. **The purge question.** The flow - config script registers the template at
    unpack, postrm asks at purge - is standard but has not been exercised. Test
    it as T8.5 says: install, use, remove (data must survive), purge (must ask,
    and must keep the data if the answer is no). Also test a purge under
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive, where the data must survive.

 7. **biokids-admin has since landed.** The lockdown unit and the daemon's
    ExecStopPost both run `/usr/bin/biokids-admin lockdown`, which was
    specified rather than observed when this packaging was written;
    biokids_core.admin now exists and provides `lockdown`, `status`,
    `sync-catalog`, `reset-pin` and `version`, and the postinst's catalogue
    seeding is a call to `biokids-admin sync-catalog` rather than an embedded
    heredoc. What is still unobserved is `biokids-admin lockdown` running
    *inside the sandbox*: it shells out to loginctl, which reaches the system
    bus through a socket under a read-only /run, and it must finish inside
    TimeoutStopSec. Test it with `systemctl kill -s KILL biokids-daemon`, which
    is what T3.7 asks for anyway.

 8. **graphical-session.target.** The user units are WantedBy it, which assumes
    XFCE's systemd session integration is active. If it is not, the session
    configuration from T5.7 has to start them explicitly, and the units'
    [Install] sections become decoration. Check with
    `systemctl --user list-units 'graphical-session*'` inside a child session.

 9. **/var/lib/biokids/evidence is 0700 root**, as specified. The screen
    watcher runs as biokids-ai and therefore cannot write there, or even
    traverse into /var/lib/biokids. That is fine today - the AI layer is Phase
    2 - and the right fix is probably for the watcher to hand frames to the
    daemon over the socket rather than for the tree to become group-traversable,
    since the daemon is already the single writer of everything else. Decide it
    with AI-LAYER.md, not here.

 10. **Upgrade behaviour.** Nothing here has ever seen a second version
    installed over a first: the conffile prompt for config.toml, the conffile
    prompt for the logrotate file, `alembic upgrade head` across a real
    migration, and a daemon left running across the upgrade. T8.5 covers all of
    it; do not skip the upgrade step, it is where packaging bugs live.
